WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.580
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

00:14.580 --> 00:18.860
Since man first looked upon the moon, he has dreamed of probing its mysteries.

00:18.860 --> 00:24.700
On a summer day in 1969, that ancient dream becomes a reality as two United States astronauts

00:24.700 --> 00:29.300
carry the stars and stripes to the Earth's natural satellite almost a quarter of a million

00:29.300 --> 00:31.400
miles from Earth.

00:31.400 --> 00:35.800
Americans are justly proud of their nation's achievements in space exploration, achievements

00:35.800 --> 00:43.640
in which the United States Army played an important early role.

00:43.640 --> 00:48.880
Beneath the sea, men of the U.S. Army move in another alien environment as Army divers

00:48.880 --> 00:54.160
go underwater to repair military facilities and salvage costly equipment sunk by accident

00:54.160 --> 00:58.060
or lost in action.

00:58.060 --> 01:03.320
Aquanaut and Aquanaut each accomplish his goal supported by an array of technical knowledge

01:03.320 --> 01:06.120
unparalleled in the history of man.

01:06.120 --> 01:11.200
Today on the big picture, the United States Army's contribution to man's conquest of

01:11.200 --> 01:35.080
the two vast and mysterious boundaries of his world, the sky and the sea.

01:35.080 --> 01:48.120
July 20, 1969, Cape Kennedy, Florida, the day of the beginning of a new era of discovery.

01:48.120 --> 01:53.960
U.S. spaceflight Apollo 11, powered by the giant Saturn launch vehicle, is about to take

01:53.960 --> 01:57.560
off for man's first landing on the moon.

01:57.560 --> 02:01.900
That giant leap for mankind began with many smaller steps.

02:01.900 --> 02:06.960
The operation of the Saturn engines powering Apollo 11 with almost 8 million pounds of

02:06.960 --> 02:13.400
thrust can be linked to experiments in rocketry conducted by the U.S. Army Air Forces 24 years

02:13.400 --> 02:22.060
earlier.

02:22.060 --> 02:27.640
In 1944, events are taking place across the Atlantic, which will have a far-reaching effect

02:27.640 --> 02:29.600
on America's space efforts.

02:29.600 --> 02:34.680
In Penemun, Germany, the Nazis continue to test their deadly V-2 missiles, which had

02:34.680 --> 02:39.240
brought destruction to England.

02:39.240 --> 02:41.480
But time ran out on the Third Reich.

02:41.480 --> 02:45.800
As Hitler tottered on the brink of defeat, the German rocket experts fled from the advancing

02:45.800 --> 02:51.640
Russians and sought out the protection of the U.S. Army.

02:51.640 --> 02:57.000
During their hectic escape, Werner von Braun, the top German missile expert, is injured

02:57.000 --> 03:03.560
in an accident, but nevertheless he and his staff reach the American forces.

03:03.560 --> 03:09.100
In an operation codenamed Paperclip, this important team of scientists and technicians is quickly

03:09.100 --> 03:15.080
transferred to Fort Bliss, Texas.

03:15.080 --> 03:21.340
By late 1945, the vast open lands at White Sands, New Mexico, close to Fort Bliss, become

03:21.340 --> 03:24.560
the main testing site for the U.S. Army missile program.

03:24.560 --> 03:30.960
As the new technology develops, the Army's White Sands Proving Ground, to be known later

03:30.960 --> 03:36.480
as White Sands Missile Range, builds facilities needed to house the complex launch and control

03:36.480 --> 03:40.600
equipment essential to the rocketry art.

03:40.600 --> 03:45.880
Army ordnance experts and the von Braun group, most of whom become U.S. citizens, conduct

03:45.880 --> 03:48.040
missile testing programs.

03:48.040 --> 03:53.920
By assimilating the German expertise and by actual firings of the V-2, America gains its

03:53.920 --> 03:59.440
first practical experience in the operation and handling of large missiles.

03:59.440 --> 04:03.920
Here began the research that heralded all of our future space effort, both civilian

04:03.920 --> 04:09.960
and military.

04:09.960 --> 04:14.440
Under operational control of the Army, White Sands is used during this period by all the

04:14.440 --> 04:17.120
services in perfecting their missiles.

04:17.120 --> 04:21.920
The U.S. Navy's Viking missile receives its early test firings here, carrying intricate

04:21.920 --> 04:27.680
electronic apparatus for research of the heavens in radio communications, weather forecasting,

04:27.680 --> 04:33.320
and high altitude flight.

04:33.320 --> 04:38.960
Air Force sleds use the vast Army range, racing at higher speeds than ever before, to learn

04:38.960 --> 04:44.800
more about human reaction to the tremendous forces that a man's body must survive as he

04:44.800 --> 04:51.700
is thrust into the vast realms of outer space.

04:51.700 --> 04:57.280
The nation's first operational surface-to-surface guided missile, the Army's Corporal, is successfully

04:57.280 --> 05:02.320
tested.

05:02.320 --> 05:07.920
The intensive space studies of the 1950s are a tribute to the work of Dr. Robert Goddard,

05:07.920 --> 05:13.280
who had conducted solid propellant rocket tests for the Army in 1918 and had fired the

05:13.280 --> 05:18.400
world's first liquid-fueled rocket as far back as 1926.

05:18.400 --> 05:23.200
Much of today's space technology evolved from the cornerstone laid by Dr. Goddard, and his

05:23.200 --> 05:28.560
contribution as a rocket pioneer is honored by the Goddard Space Flight Center in Maryland,

05:28.560 --> 05:33.480
which bears his name.

05:33.480 --> 05:38.480
In the decade following World War II, the Army almost alone pursued the advancement

05:38.480 --> 05:41.880
of rocket-propelled missiles and space vehicles.

05:41.880 --> 05:47.040
For a while, it was virtually a shoestring operation, with only token budgets from the

05:47.040 --> 05:48.480
government.

05:48.480 --> 05:53.960
Still the Army managed to continue its research and development until, during the Korean War,

05:53.960 --> 05:58.720
additional funds become available to back the Army's effort to keep America ahead in

05:58.720 --> 06:01.840
space technology.

06:01.840 --> 06:12.600
Gradually, the new technology shapes the pattern of things to come.

06:12.600 --> 06:17.560
Saving devices, specially designed camera, and a variety of telemetric equipment are

06:17.560 --> 06:25.000
devised under Army supervision to analyze the performance of projectiles hurled skyward.

06:25.000 --> 06:31.580
Each step in the perfection of military missile systems points inevitably in the direction

06:31.580 --> 06:40.600
that man will take to the stars.

06:40.600 --> 06:52.680
In test ranges, rocket engines undergo prolonged static firings to determine their efficiency.

06:52.680 --> 06:58.840
In April 1950, the Army's rocket experts were moved to the Redstone Arsenal in Alabama,

06:58.840 --> 07:04.600
and thereafter, Redstone becomes the chief center for rocket and guided missile research.

07:04.600 --> 07:13.360
Here, named for its birthplace, there was created the powerful Redstone missile, a weapon

07:13.360 --> 07:23.520
that was to play a vital role in our steps to the moon.

07:23.520 --> 07:29.000
Following Redstone, fueled by liquid propellants, the Army began the development of solid-fuel

07:29.000 --> 07:35.000
rocket engines and missiles that could be emplaced and fired without delay.

07:35.000 --> 07:45.720
First of these was Sargent, highly mobile, quickly emplaced, and extremely accurate.

07:45.720 --> 07:54.000
Next came Pershing, big, powerful, highly mobile, easily moved by air, range 400 miles.

07:54.000 --> 07:59.160
Later, a wheeled transporter is fitted to the Pershing 1A so that it can travel across

07:59.160 --> 08:02.440
country under its own power.

08:02.440 --> 08:10.080
But Redstone, Sargent, and Pershing were only a part of a whole series of rocket-powered

08:10.080 --> 08:17.320
Army weapons developed by the Army Missile Command.

08:17.320 --> 08:20.120
Some like Little John.

08:20.120 --> 08:25.240
And Honest John were simple free-flight rockets designed for artillery use in battlefield

08:25.240 --> 08:26.800
areas.

08:26.800 --> 08:42.680
Others, like Dart, were guided by wire and were directed by the gunner.

08:42.680 --> 08:52.920
And Shillelagh, that would become the main striking force for America's armored vehicles.

08:52.920 --> 08:57.840
Or Dragon, the free world's first assault missile, light enough to be carried by one

08:57.840 --> 09:12.240
soldier, yet powerful enough to destroy armor and other enemy strong points.

09:12.240 --> 09:18.080
For air defense against high-flying aircraft, the Army provided the Nike Ajax.

09:18.080 --> 09:29.840
Then followed the longer-range, fast-reaction Nike Hercules.

09:29.840 --> 09:41.160
Later, work began on protection against intercontinental missile attack.

09:41.160 --> 09:47.120
First in the new family of longer-range missiles was the Nike Zeus.

09:47.120 --> 10:01.440
Today, Nike Zeus has become the Spartan missile.

10:01.440 --> 10:05.520
And Spartan has been teamed with the high-acceleration Sprint missile.

10:05.520 --> 10:10.200
Together, these two highly accurate weapons are part of the safeguard ballistic missile

10:10.200 --> 10:14.360
defense system.

10:14.360 --> 10:26.960
To shield against low-flying enemy aircraft, there was Hawk, used by both U.S. and Allied

10:26.960 --> 10:32.920
forces around the world.

10:32.920 --> 10:39.000
And for the infantrymen, Red Eye, a portable shoulder-fired guided missile designed to

10:39.000 --> 10:45.240
destroy by homing on the heat of a jet engine.

10:45.240 --> 10:50.680
But while the Army's prime objective was defense of our nation, the vast and rapidly growing

10:50.680 --> 10:56.040
fund of rocketry knowledge that the Army accumulated was to become America's foundation for the

10:56.040 --> 10:59.200
peaceful exploration of outer space.

10:59.200 --> 11:06.000
A massive output of data on rocket propulsion, performance, and control is filed away, indexed,

11:06.000 --> 11:15.600
and made available for research.

11:15.600 --> 11:23.920
America learns more about rocket fuels, more about nose cone design to survive the fiery

11:23.920 --> 11:31.160
plunge back into the Earth's atmosphere, more about self-contained guidance systems that

11:31.160 --> 11:43.380
can steer a vehicle unerringly through all types of weather and atmospheric conditions.

11:43.380 --> 11:49.160
And of particular importance, more about designing engines powerful enough to lift a man-made

11:49.160 --> 11:54.300
satellite beyond the pull of gravity.

11:54.300 --> 12:00.680
This Saturn booster, first tested by the Army in 1958, was one of several dramatic accomplishments

12:00.680 --> 12:07.160
that would lead to man's first steps on the moon.

12:07.160 --> 12:11.800
Several other key milestones on the road to Project Apollo also took place in the late

12:11.800 --> 12:12.800
1950s.

12:12.800 --> 12:17.400
An Army Jupiter-C missile is chosen to put into orbit the free world's first scientific

12:17.400 --> 12:22.200
satellite, which becomes the historic Explorer 1.

12:22.200 --> 12:27.320
Explorer 1 will radio back data on the radiation belt surrounding the Earth.

12:27.320 --> 12:35.680
Such indispensable to manned space flight.

12:35.680 --> 12:41.600
Shortly afterward, another Army satellite, Pioneer 4, will become the first successful

12:41.600 --> 12:48.880
American lunar probe, passing within 33,000 miles of the moon and sending back more information

12:48.880 --> 12:52.600
essential to future space exploration.

12:52.600 --> 12:57.960
In May of 1959, America achieves a historic breakthrough.

12:57.960 --> 13:04.040
A Jupiter missile launches a capsule into space containing monkeys, Abel and Baker.

13:04.040 --> 13:10.120
Their journey 300 miles up and 1,200 miles downrange from Cape Canaveral ends in their

13:10.120 --> 13:13.960
live recovery, one hour after liftoff.

13:13.960 --> 13:19.080
Living breathing animals have voyaged safely into the dark regions of outer space and back

13:19.080 --> 13:21.160
again.

13:21.160 --> 13:25.880
Throughout this series of successful space launches, Army spokesmen take the lead in

13:25.880 --> 13:32.080
urging a national space program.

13:32.080 --> 13:37.200
Major General John B. Medeiros, head of the Army Ballistic Missile Agency at Redstone,

13:37.200 --> 13:42.560
and Dr. Von Braun, now Chief of Development and Operations, suggest that the country could

13:42.560 --> 13:48.640
and should begin a full-scale program of space exploration.

13:48.640 --> 13:53.120
A separate civilian space agency is created by executive order of President Dwight D.

13:53.120 --> 13:55.120
Eisenhower.

13:55.120 --> 13:59.560
Many Army scientists involved in the early rocket studies at White Sands and Redstone

13:59.560 --> 14:06.880
move into the new organization, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, NASA.

14:06.880 --> 14:11.600
Secretary of the Army Wilbur Bruecker guides and encourages the Army's historic pioneering

14:11.600 --> 14:18.760
efforts in the exciting days at the beginning of the missile and space era.

14:18.760 --> 14:24.600
May 5, 1961, is the start of U.S. manned space flights.

14:24.600 --> 14:29.720
Commander Alan Shepard becomes the first American in space.

14:29.720 --> 14:35.400
Secured in a capsule atop a modified Army Redstone missile, Commander Shepard is rocketed

14:35.400 --> 14:47.640
115 miles up and 302 miles downrange from Cape Canaveral for a successful mission.

14:47.640 --> 14:52.840
Thus did history record the United States Army's early leadership of America's adventure

14:52.840 --> 14:56.040
into space.

14:56.040 --> 15:03.320
Then, with NASA established, the Army continues to concentrate on the military applications

15:03.320 --> 15:05.840
of using the space environment.

15:05.840 --> 15:11.360
Army agencies develop new electronic communications for the growing family of missile systems.

15:11.360 --> 15:16.400
The Signal Corps moves ahead in communications research, not only in missilery, but in the

15:16.400 --> 15:21.760
gradually developing field of satellite communications for linking widely deployed troop units in

15:21.760 --> 15:22.760
the field.

15:22.760 --> 15:29.680
As far back as 1946, the Signal Corps, in Operation Diana, had bounced radar signals

15:29.680 --> 15:37.120
off the moon, demonstrating that communications throughout our space were possible.

15:37.120 --> 15:43.400
In 1958, Project Score, the world's first orbiting communications satellite developed

15:43.400 --> 15:49.520
at Fort Monmouth Laboratories, transmits the voice of President Eisenhower around the world,

15:49.520 --> 15:52.400
a Christmas message that literally comes from the sky.

15:52.400 --> 15:59.920
To the men, women, and children of America, and to all peoples throughout the world, a

15:59.920 --> 16:04.560
Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year.

16:04.560 --> 16:10.080
Now Signal Corps scientists pioneer in a whole new field of modernized communications built

16:10.080 --> 16:16.400
around the concept of orbiting satellites.

16:16.400 --> 16:22.200
To fully explore the potentials in space communication, the U.S. Army Satellite Communications Agency

16:22.200 --> 16:28.280
is created in September 1960.

16:28.280 --> 16:33.880
In the years that follow, SATCOM develops programs for both tactical and strategic satellite

16:33.880 --> 16:41.600
communications and becomes a leading participant in the nation's space program.

16:41.600 --> 16:49.920
SATCOM's tactical ground terminals consist of various types of radar receiver and transmission

16:49.920 --> 16:59.920
equipment.

16:59.920 --> 17:04.540
The nation's communications satellites are launched by Air Force missiles.

17:04.540 --> 17:08.960
Orbiting high above the earth, they carry important Department of Defense messages 24

17:08.960 --> 17:12.600
hours a day.

17:12.600 --> 17:17.640
The Army, through the Satellite Communications Agency, is responsible for providing the ground

17:17.640 --> 17:25.520
environment for the Military Satellite Communications Program.

17:25.520 --> 17:30.800
Deployed around the world, tactical satellite communications terminals can get the message

17:30.800 --> 17:36.920
through, whether the distance is across a hill or thousands of miles across an ocean.

17:36.920 --> 17:42.000
SATCOM tactical terminals were instrumental in relaying communications data via satellite

17:42.000 --> 17:48.080
for safe recovery of the astronauts on their return from space.

17:48.080 --> 17:53.360
Today, like other Army agencies, the Satellite Communications Agency continues to provide

17:53.360 --> 17:57.480
support for our country's space explorations.

17:57.480 --> 18:02.360
At its Fort Monmouth headquarters, banks of telemetric equipment monitor SATCOM's hundreds

18:02.360 --> 18:04.520
of orbiting satellites.

18:04.520 --> 18:09.600
These not only carry vast amounts of information, but provide television transmission around

18:09.600 --> 18:15.200
the world, including pictures from Apollo moon missions.

18:15.200 --> 18:20.280
Personnel skilled in communications electronics tune in on the satellite broadcasts to check

18:20.280 --> 18:25.720
the efficiency of the signal and to make certain that all satellite stations are operating.

18:25.720 --> 18:31.640
Control, Control, this is TRICOM 8-5, over.

18:31.640 --> 18:39.520
Control, this is 8-5, can you give me position of satellite less six, over.

18:39.520 --> 18:44.280
Control, Roger, 8-5, over.

18:44.280 --> 18:49.600
Operations in space demand the concentrated efforts of many types of scientific skills.

18:49.600 --> 18:53.720
Manned space exploration requires very special planning in the preparation of navigational

18:53.720 --> 18:55.040
maps.

18:55.040 --> 19:10.120
Map making for the U.S. space program is one of the activities of the U.S. Army Topographic

19:10.120 --> 19:11.120
Command.

19:11.120 --> 19:16.160
In a special topographic command project for NASA, Army scientists map the lunar surface

19:16.160 --> 19:18.280
for the early moon landings.

19:18.280 --> 19:24.880
Now they continue the increasingly important job of charting space.

19:24.880 --> 19:28.440
Information from aerial photographs taken by space satellites.

19:28.440 --> 19:33.360
Pictures of the moon are projected in fragments and assembled into a large reproduction of

19:33.360 --> 19:40.960
a section of the lunar surface.

19:40.960 --> 19:46.800
A giant plastic simulation of the moon's surface, made by Army map experts, provided a practical

19:46.800 --> 19:50.680
training facility for the first landings on the moon.

19:50.680 --> 20:07.360
Later map making has proved invaluable to astronauts in the APAVO program.

20:07.360 --> 20:12.120
To learn more about how man and equipment will function in space, scientists and engineers

20:12.120 --> 20:14.120
have gone underwater.

20:14.120 --> 20:18.520
The weightlessness of outer space is simulated in this neutral buoyancy tank at the Marshall

20:18.520 --> 20:21.040
Space Flight Center in Alabama.

20:21.040 --> 20:26.800
Inside the tank, astronauts practice activities they will have to perform during their flights.

20:26.800 --> 20:31.680
Some of the space hardware, clothing, and foods they use are developed by Army scientists

20:31.680 --> 20:40.640
in cooperation with NASA.

20:40.640 --> 20:45.400
The Army has designed a special environmental test chamber, which is being used at White

20:45.400 --> 20:47.640
Sands Missile Range.

20:47.640 --> 20:52.160
The astronauts' space suits are tested to see how well they can withstand the extremes

20:52.160 --> 21:00.240
of heat and cold that are encountered on the surface of the moon.

21:00.240 --> 21:05.440
The panorama of space exploration and the U.S. Army's contribution to the program is

21:05.440 --> 21:12.080
dramatically on display at the Alabama Space and Rocket Center in Huntsville.

21:12.080 --> 21:23.960
Astronauts' earliest dreams of flight are portrayed in works of art.

21:23.960 --> 21:29.240
Realistic exhibits, activated by visitors, illustrate many principles involved in space

21:29.240 --> 21:34.160
flight.

21:34.160 --> 21:39.000
The gyroscopic chair suggests the spinning action astronauts might feel as they rocket

21:39.000 --> 21:48.240
through space.

21:48.240 --> 21:52.920
Much of the research in stabilizing and guidance systems needed for space travel was evolved

21:52.920 --> 22:07.560
from early Army missile guidance systems.

22:07.560 --> 22:11.680
One view at the center is a replica of the capsule in which Commander Shepard made the

22:11.680 --> 22:19.160
country's first space journey atop the Army redstone missile, an impressive array of various

22:19.160 --> 22:33.400
stages in the progress of space flight.

22:33.400 --> 22:40.960
Milestones in Army development of the projectile art are vividly displayed in an outdoor exhibit.

22:40.960 --> 22:46.080
This early effort in space technology provided the nation with its first exploration in a

22:46.080 --> 22:47.780
new dimension.

22:47.780 --> 22:52.960
In devising a body of rocketry knowledge that opened a window into a new world, U.S. Army

22:52.960 --> 22:57.480
scientists laid the groundwork for the brilliant achievements of the National Aeronautics and

22:57.480 --> 23:07.160
Space Administration, which they continue to support.

23:07.160 --> 23:13.320
Beneath the sea, U.S. Army skill moves in at other strange and challenging frontier,

23:13.320 --> 23:19.480
as Army divers train for the difficult and dangerous job of underwater salvage and repair.

23:19.480 --> 23:24.720
At Fort Yustis, Virginia, the Army Divers School provides intensive instruction in how

23:24.720 --> 23:29.640
to maneuver and work in an underwater environment.

23:29.640 --> 23:35.760
A select group of young men in excellent physical condition receives a 26-week course in three

23:35.760 --> 23:41.560
forms of diving, shallow water, scuba, and deep sea.

23:41.560 --> 23:46.200
The mission of the Army diving team is to salvage sunken equipment and repair underwater

23:46.200 --> 23:51.600
facilities such as pipelines or pier supports.

23:51.600 --> 23:56.200
A working model of a recompression chamber is used to determine the student's reaction

23:56.200 --> 24:09.800
to underwater pressure at various depths.

24:09.800 --> 24:14.380
Controls on the training apparatus create pressure conditions inside the chamber similar

24:14.380 --> 24:18.280
to those the diver may encounter underwater.

24:18.280 --> 24:23.380
The recompression chamber is designed essentially for treatment of divers suffering from compressed

24:23.380 --> 24:27.320
air illness, sometimes known as the bends.

24:27.320 --> 24:33.680
A diver breathing compressed air beyond the depth of 35 feet is subject to this condition.

24:33.680 --> 24:38.480
Relief is provided in the chamber by simulating the pressure at the diver's original depth

24:38.480 --> 24:41.000
and then gradually reducing it.

24:41.000 --> 24:56.160
Instruction in metal cutting and welding prepares the student for underwater salvage and repair

24:56.160 --> 25:00.920
operations.

25:00.920 --> 25:11.040
Training is also provided in underwater demolition.

25:11.040 --> 25:15.680
Before the students are assigned tasks in actual waterways, they receive practice training

25:15.680 --> 25:20.600
in a specially designed tank.

25:20.600 --> 25:25.640
In this exchange, they will learn how to remove a disabled propeller from a ship.

25:25.640 --> 25:28.120
Student divers face rigorous training.

25:28.120 --> 25:32.240
Only the best can make it through the course and qualify as full-fledged members of the

25:32.240 --> 25:51.000
Army Diving Team.

25:51.000 --> 25:55.540
With classroom and tank training completed, the student diver gets a chance to put it

25:55.540 --> 26:00.000
all into practice in actual underwater problems.

26:00.000 --> 26:03.640
Home base is the Army Diver's school training barge.

26:03.640 --> 26:07.880
A student descends to make an underwater survey.

26:07.880 --> 26:11.680
Materials have been placed at the bottom of the water near the barge.

26:11.680 --> 26:16.920
The student diver must report to his instructor exactly what he sees below and make an appraisal

26:16.920 --> 26:18.720
of the salvage or maintenance problem.

26:18.720 --> 26:21.640
I want you to go on and analyze the situation down there and tell me just what the bottom

26:21.640 --> 26:22.640
condition is.

26:22.640 --> 26:26.880
And when you get onto the steel pipe, I want you to go out and check it out and tell me

26:26.880 --> 26:33.120
exactly what you're going to need in order to do this job.

26:33.120 --> 26:37.980
Army divers are responsible for clearance and maintenance of inland waterways and harbors

26:37.980 --> 26:41.160
used by our military units around the world.

26:41.160 --> 26:46.920
The first salvage diving school was formed at Fort Scriven's, Georgia in 1943 to furnish

26:46.920 --> 26:51.140
divers for port construction units in World War II.

26:51.140 --> 26:56.800
From Antwerp to Naples, from Port Moresby to Manila Bay, Army divers went below the

26:56.800 --> 27:01.480
sea to make certain that the waters were clear for navigation.

27:01.480 --> 27:06.760
Following World War II, the salvage diving school was transferred to Fort Eustis.

27:06.760 --> 27:11.400
In the Korean War, another urgent need for divers to clear the ports of entry drew on

27:11.400 --> 27:15.800
the skills of graduates from the Fort Eustis training facility.

27:15.800 --> 27:20.940
The Army Diver's mission in Vietnam salvaged millions of dollars' worth of equipment, kept

27:20.940 --> 27:26.440
underwater pipelines in repair, helped build docks, and secured the harbor environment

27:26.440 --> 27:30.760
for safe passage of transport and supply vessels.

27:30.760 --> 27:38.080
A major test of Army diver training was participation of members of the Army Diving Team in Operation

27:38.080 --> 27:42.560
Tektite II off the Virgin Islands in 1970.

27:42.560 --> 27:46.880
The purpose of the program was to test man's ability to survive and work beneath the sea

27:46.880 --> 27:49.740
for long periods of time.

27:49.740 --> 27:55.200
Military divers brought supplies down to the aquanauts and provided for underwater safety.

27:55.200 --> 27:59.360
Should an aquanaut's breathing apparatus become fouled, an accident occur in their

27:59.360 --> 28:04.980
undersea quarters, or shark or barracuda threaten, the divers would be immediately available

28:04.980 --> 28:08.000
for rescue operations.

28:08.000 --> 28:14.220
Whether the effort is deep beneath the sea or in the farthest reaches of outer space,

28:14.220 --> 28:19.400
it is supported by the highly skilled and inventive scientific team of the United States

28:19.400 --> 28:25.440
Army, an Army constantly in search of new horizons for the advancement of our nation.

